4n&=m^2- m \\ \Rightarrow\qquad n&=\frac{m^2- m}{4}\\ \Rightarrow\qquad n&=\frac{1}{4}\left(m-\frac{1}{2}\right)^2-\frac{1}{16}.
\end{align*}
Note that the only possible value of $n$ that satisfies this equation must be in the range $-\frac14 < n$, since $m^2- m$ must be a non-negative number (since it is equal to 4 times a square). We can clear this out by letting $m=0$. Thus, $n$ must be equal to $\boxed{-\frac14}$. </think> <answer> -\frac14 </answer>

